WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.580 --> 00:00:06.920
We are now ready to find the most likely
hidden path in an HMM, and it corresponds

00:00:06.920 --> 00:00:12.360
to the following decoding problem that
asks us to find the optimal hidden path.

00:00:12.360 --> 00:00:15.030
The input is emitted string and

00:00:15.030 --> 00:00:19.560
an HMM defined by four objects:
alphabet, the set of states,

00:00:19.560 --> 00:00:24.680
the matrix of transition probabilities,
and the matrix of emission probabilities.

00:00:24.680 --> 00:00:31.930
And the output is a hidden pass pi that
maximizes the probability of x and pi.

00:00:34.170 --> 00:00:39.699
Pr(x,pi), as we saw,
is equal to the product

00:00:39.699 --> 00:00:43.105
Pr(x|pi) * Pr(pi),

00:00:43.105 --> 00:00:48.105
and it is the product of n terms,
each term being the product of

00:00:48.105 --> 00:00:54.593
emission probability and transition
probability as described in the slide.

00:00:54.593 --> 00:00:56.807
To solve the decoding problem,

00:00:56.807 --> 00:01:01.235
we will try to build a Manhattan
graph in such a way that every path

00:01:01.235 --> 00:01:06.510
in this graph will correspond to
a hidden path in the HMM and vice versa:

00:01:06.510 --> 00:01:12.566
every hidden path in the HMM will
correspond to a path in the built Manhattan.

00:01:12.566 --> 00:01:16.351
For the coin flipping HMM
with this HMM diagram,

00:01:16.351 --> 00:01:20.402
we will construct a Manhattan
consisting of two rows and

00:01:20.402 --> 00:01:24.200
n columns,
where n is the number emitted symbols.

00:01:25.850 --> 00:01:30.030
In this case, we'll also add source and
sink nodes to the beginning and

00:01:30.030 --> 00:01:34.580
to the end of the resulting
directed acyclic graph,

00:01:34.580 --> 00:01:40.263
and for the HMMs that emitted
the string of symbols F B B B F F,

00:01:40.263 --> 00:01:47.630
we will construct the following path
through the Manhattan right here.

00:01:47.630 --> 00:01:51.160
And you see that for
every string of emitted symbols,

00:01:51.160 --> 00:01:55.510
we can construct the path in
the corresponding Manhattan.

00:01:55.510 --> 00:02:00.130
For a little bit more complex
HMM consisting of three states,

00:02:00.130 --> 00:02:06.250
our Manhattan will be built
of three rows and n columns.

00:02:06.250 --> 00:02:13.380
Generally, if we have a HMM with k states,
there will k rows in this Manhattan.

00:02:13.380 --> 00:02:17.269
As before, we will add start and
end states and

00:02:17.269 --> 00:02:22.157
note that in difference from
the Alignment Manhattan,

00:02:22.157 --> 00:02:26.047
when we had just three
directions from every node,

00:02:26.047 --> 00:02:30.357
in the Decoding Manhattan
we have many directions:

00:02:30.357 --> 00:02:37.230
anything that goes to the east works
in this newly built Manhattan.

00:02:38.590 --> 00:02:42.632
We now need to define
the weights of edges in

00:02:42.632 --> 00:02:46.799
the newly built Manhattan graph
for an HMM.

00:02:46.799 --> 00:02:53.536
Let's consider an edge that starts
in column i-1 in state l and

00:02:53.536 --> 00:03:00.288
connects it to the node corresponding
to state k in the column i.

00:03:00.288 --> 00:03:06.057
We will refer to this edge as (l,k,i-1).

00:03:07.400 --> 00:03:12.152
Now, this edge corresponds to
transitioning from state l

00:03:12.152 --> 00:03:16.013
to state k with probability transition_(l,k),

00:03:16.013 --> 00:03:22.270
and it also corresponds to emitting
the symbol xi from the state k.

00:03:22.270 --> 00:03:26.754
And therefore, we define the weight

00:03:26.754 --> 00:03:31.243
of this edge as weight_(l,k,i-pi),

00:03:31.243 --> 00:03:37.223
and it is equal to the product
of the emission probability

00:03:37.223 --> 00:03:42.508
of emitting symbol xi
from state k multiplied

00:03:42.508 --> 00:03:48.501
by the transition probability
from state l to state k.

00:03:48.501 --> 00:03:55.305
Let's see how we would assign edge weight
for the crooked casino.

00:03:55.305 --> 00:04:00.811
In this case, as before, the weight of
edge (l,k,i-1) is to equal to

00:04:00.811 --> 00:04:05.812
the product of the corresponding
emission and transition probabilities.

00:04:05.812 --> 00:04:07.353
What about this edge?

00:04:07.353 --> 00:04:12.595
So the weight of this bold edge,
from state B to state B,

00:04:12.595 --> 00:04:17.729
is equal to the emission probability
of emitting symbol H from state B

00:04:17.729 --> 00:04:23.409
multiplied by the transition
probability of moving from B to B,

00:04:23.409 --> 00:04:27.252
which is 0.75 * 0.9.

00:04:27.252 --> 00:04:30.897
And we will continue
further with assigning

00:04:30.897 --> 00:04:35.337
the probability of all
edges in a similar fashion,

00:04:35.337 --> 00:04:40.645
and afterwards,
we will construct a graph in which

00:04:40.645 --> 00:04:47.682
product weight of a hidden path will
be simply equal to the product of

00:04:47.682 --> 00:04:54.520
weights of individual edges
as shown on this slide.

00:04:54.520 --> 00:04:59.270
For example,
the product weight of this path

00:04:59.270 --> 00:05:03.770
will be simply equal to Pr(x,pi).

00:05:04.890 --> 00:05:10.546
Therefore, we constructed a graph in
which the product weight of every path

00:05:10.546 --> 00:05:16.110
is equal to Pr(x,pi),
exactly what we're interested in.

00:05:16.110 --> 00:05:20.974
And therefore,
the computing of Pr(x,pi)

00:05:20.974 --> 00:05:26.734
and finding a hidden path pi corresponding
to maximizing Pr(x,pi)

00:05:26.734 --> 00:05:31.518
is simply finding a path
with maximum product weight.

